---
/*
 * OtherWays — the paths for people who don't want a one-liner.
 *
 * THE ENV-VAR FORM ON THIS PAGE IS DELIBERATELY NOT THE ONE install.sh PRINTS.
 * install.sh's header and two of its error messages suggest
 *     CSWARM_INSTALL_DIR=$HOME/.local/bin curl -fsSL <url>/install.sh | sh
 * and that does not work: a variable assignment prefixed to a command exports it into THAT
 * command's environment — curl's — and the `sh` on the other side of the pipe is a separate
 * process that never sees it. The assignment has to prefix `sh`. The working form is what is
 * shown here; the script itself is worth fixing separately (reported, not silently papered
 * over — the page must not print a command that doesn't do what it says).
 *
 * THE TWO INSTALLER VARIABLES ARE RENAMED, AND THE SCRIPT WAS READ TO CONFIRM IT.
 * CommonSwarm's rename turns COSWARM_VERSION / COSWARM_INSTALL_DIR into CSWARM_VERSION /
 * CSWARM_INSTALL_DIR, and install.sh reads exactly those two names at l.13 and l.14. This
 * mattered enough to check rather than assume: a variable the script does not read is not an
 * error, it is silence — the installer would fall through to its defaults and the reader
 * would never learn the flag did nothing. (SWARM_CLOUD_URL and SWARM_CLOUD_ANON_KEY are a
 * different family and keep their names; so does SWARM_SELF_SERVE.)
 *
 * npm: there is NO published package. registry.npmjs.org/coswarm and /cloud-swarm were both
 * checked and both answer 404 — but that was the OLD name. `cswarm` has NOT been checked
 * against the registry by this pass, so the card no longer names which strings 404; it says
 * only that no package is published, which is a fact about our own release process rather
 * than about a namespace nobody measured. Do not put a name back into that copy without
 * curling the registry for it first — the whole risk is that someone else already holds it.
 *
 * From source: verified by doing it. `npm run build` is `tsc` (package.json) and the bin entry
 * is dist/cli.js — package.json now declares bin as {"cswarm": "dist/cli.js"}, so the command
 * name the reader ends up with is the renamed one. `node dist/cli.js --version` printed
 * "coswarm 0.0.1 (protocol 0.1.0)" when it was last actually run, i.e. before the rename;
 * src/cli.ts:248 now composes that line with a `cswarm` prefix. Not re-run here.
 *
 * THE REPOSITORY IS PUBLIC. This card used to say it was "private while coswarm is in invited
 * dogfood" (the product's name at the time; it is CommonSwarm now), which was false and was
 * the last place on the site still saying it — SiteFooter,
 * index, download and Invite had all already been corrected. Checked on this invocation:
 * `curl -s https://api.github.com/repos/Ridge-io/cloud-swarm` answers `"private": false,
 * "visibility": "public"`, and an anonymous GET of the HTML page answers 200 (GitHub answers
 * 404 to anonymous visitors on a private repo, so the 200 is the control). The clone URL in
 * this card's own code block, the header's GitHub icon and the footer's PROJECT column all
 * already pointed at it. Do not reintroduce the private claim without re-running that curl.
 *
 * Do not put a line count in visible copy. The installer grew while this page was being
 * written, and a precise count turned into a false promise without changing the behavior a
 * reader cares about. It remains short, commented POSIX sh and can be read before it runs.
 */

import Card from "../ui/Card.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../ui/CodeBlock.astro";
import Badge from "../ui/Badge.astro";
import { INSTALL_CMD_DIR } from "../../lib/install.ts";

interface Props {
  /** The same install command shown at the top of the page. */
  cmd: string;
}

const { cmd } = Astro.props;

/* Derived from the one command, so a change at the top of the page cannot leave this
   variant behind pointing at a different host. */
const readCmd = cmd.replace(/\|\s*sh$/, "| less");
const pinCmd = cmd.replace(/\|\s*sh$/, "| CSWARM_VERSION=0.1.3 sh");
const dirCmd = INSTALL_CMD_DIR;
const sourceCmd = `git clone https://github.com/Ridge-io/cloud-swarm.git
cd cloud-swarm
npm install
npm run build
node dist/cli.js --version`;
---

<section class="ow section--tight" id="other-ways" aria-labelledby="ow-title">
  <div class="page">
    <div class="ow__head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Other ways in</p>
      <h2 class="h-section" id="ow-title">If a one-liner isn’t your style.</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="ow__grid reveal-stagger">
      <Card as="article" title="Read it first" headingLevel={3} class="ow__card">
        <p class="ow__p">
          The installer is short, commented POSIX sh with each decision explained. Nothing in
          it is minified and nothing is fetched from a second host.
        </p>
        <CodeBlock code={readCmd} label="read" prompt="$" wrap />
      </Card>

      <Card as="article" title="npm" headingLevel={3} class="ow__card">
        <div class="ow__status">
          <Badge variant="neutral" size="sm">Not published</Badge>
        </div>
        <p class="ow__p ow__p--last">
          There is no npm package yet, so there is no command to give you here. Publishing one
          is a decision that hasn’t been made. Until it is, the installer and the source tree
          are the two real paths, and this card would rather say so than print an
          <span class="mono">npm i -g</span> line that installs nothing.
        </p>
      </Card>

      <Card as="article" title="Pin a version, or pick the directory" headingLevel={3} class="ow__card">
        <p class="ow__p">
          <span class="mono">CSWARM_VERSION</span> installs a specific release instead of the
          latest. <span class="mono">CSWARM_INSTALL_DIR</span> puts the file somewhere other
          than <span class="mono">~/.local/bin</span>. The assignment goes on
          <span class="mono">sh</span>, not on <span class="mono">curl</span> — the two sides
          of a pipe are separate processes.
        </p>
        <CodeBlock code={pinCmd} label="pin a version" prompt="$" wrap />
        <CodeBlock code={dirCmd} label="choose a directory" prompt="$" wrap class="ow__second" />
      </Card>

      <Card as="article" title="From source" headingLevel={3} class="ow__card">
        <p class="ow__p">
          Needs Node 24+ and a clone of the repository. The build is plain
          <span class="mono">tsc</span>; the result is the same single file the installer
          would have fetched.
        </p>
        {/* wrap: the clone URL is wider than this card below ~1100px and was being clipped
            inside the block’s own scroller. Each line here starts with its own prompt, so a
            wrapped line still reads as a continuation rather than as a new command. */}
        <CodeBlock code={sourceCmd} label="build it yourself" prompt="$" wrap />
      </Card>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .ow__head {
    max-width: 44rem;
    margin-inline: auto;
    text-align: center;
  }

  .ow__head .h-section {
    margin: var(--s-4) 0 0;
  }

  .ow__grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: var(--gap-grid);
    margin-top: var(--s-12);
    /* Stretch, not start. The two cards in a row differ in height by roughly a code block,
       and `start` left a card-sized hole under the shorter one. Pairing a short card with a
       tall one in each row (npm beside "read it first", source beside the env vars) keeps the
       stretched height from being mostly empty. */
    align-items: stretch;
  }

  .ow__p {
    margin: 0 0 var(--s-5);
    font-size: var(--t-sm);
    line-height: var(--lh-base);
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  /* A card whose last child is a paragraph has no code block to sit against the padding. */
  .ow__p--last {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  .ow__status {
    margin-bottom: var(--s-4);
  }

  .ow__second {
    margin-top: var(--s-3);
  }

  @media (max-width: 56rem) {
    .ow__grid {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
      max-width: 42rem;
      margin-inline: auto;
    }
  }
</style>
